    "reviewText": "I'll start by reading the buildwright framework files and the requirement brief, then examine the working-tree diff.## `/bw-review` — independent code + security review\n\n**Target:** uncommitted working-tree changes on `bugfix/p2-buy-button-stays-enabled-quotes-payout` vs `master` (merge-base `024afc830b`)\n**Diff:** 4 production files + 4 spec files (+305/−14); 2 untracked doc trees (`docs/spec-to-pr/`, `.spec-to-pr/`)\n**Brief:** `docs/spec-to-pr/specs/2026-08-26-p2-…-design.md` (issue #1245)\n\n---\n\n## LENS 1 — Requirement coverage\n\nThe brief is short; its normative sentence is: *\"The Buy control should either cap the stake at the available balance **or** disable Buy with an inline reason when the entered stake exceeds the balance.\"* The \"Actual\" paragraph adds the symptoms (Buy enabled, payout quoted, no cap, no warning). No non-goals are declared. Requirements derived independently of the plan:\n\n| # | Requirement (derived from the brief) | Status | Evidence |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| R1 | When the stake exceeds the available balance, Buy must not stay enabled (cap **or** disable — implementer's choice) — **AppV2** | **COVERED** | New `has_trade_param_errors` gate, `purchase-button.tsx:122,218-224`; independent of `proposal_info`, so it holds even if a quote survives |\n| R2 | Same, **desktop / legacy mobile-web** | **COVERED** | Store rule `validation-rules.ts:22-35` → `requestProposal()` wipe branch `trade-store.ts:1589-1598` → `!info.id` → `purchase.tsx:92`. Verified the wipe is actually reached at boot: `processNewValuesAsync` runs `validateAllProperties()` *before* `debouncedProposal()` when `currency`/`contract_type` are in the new state (`trade-store.ts:1512-1516`), so the first proposal request already sees the error |\n| R3 | An **inline reason** is shown wherever Buy is disabled — desktop | **COVERED** | `validation_errors.amount` already rendered under the stake input (`Form/TradeParams/amount.tsx:112,155,181`) |\n| R4 | Inline reason — AppV2 stake action sheet | **COVERED** | `insufficient_balance_error` in the `message`/`status` precedence chains and in the `onSave` guard (`stake-input.tsx:176-181, 386, 415-432`) |\n| R5 | Inline reason — AppV2 trade page (where Buy actually lives) | **COVERED** | Snackbar bypass (`trade-error-snackbar.tsx:25`, `useTradeError.ts:27`) + red outline on the collapsed Stake tile (`stake.tsx:40-41`). This is the part the plan under-scoped and the implementation correctly added: without it, two-button types (Rise/Fall, Digits) would have gone dead silently, because the proposal wipe closes the `is_all_types_with_errors` gate |\n| R6 | No payout quoted on the Buy control for an unaffordable stake | **COVERED** | Proposal wiped ⇒ `info = {}` ⇒ `PurchaseButtonContent` renders the empty placeholder, not a stale payout (`purchase-button-content.tsx:41-51`) — and not a perpetual spinner |\n| R7 | Error must clear again when the situation resolves (deposit / balance rise) — implied by \"when the stake exceeds\" being a state, not an event | **COVERED** | Balance/login reaction `trade-store.ts:701-711`, re-requests the proposal on error-state transitions only; unit-tested in both directions plus logout |\n| R8 | Boundary: stake **equal** to balance must remain tradable | **COVERED** | Strict `>`; tested at rule level and sheet level |\n| R9 | Logged-out and unknown-balance users unaffected | **COVERED** | `condition` requires `is_logged_in`; `!Number.isFinite(balance)` ⇒ rule passes |\n\n**No PARTIAL or MISSING in-scope requirements.** One residue is discussed under code review (Rec-2): the stake sheet still renders the proposal's payout next to the error, which is literally the \"a payout of $7.69 is quoted\" symptom — but on the entry surface, not on the Buy control the brief's Expected sentence governs, and Save is blocked so it cannot be committed. I classify that as advisory rather than a coverage gap.\n\n---\n\n## SECURITY REVIEW (security-engineer persona)\n\n### Verdict: ✅ SECURE\n\n**Automated scans**\n- Dependency vulnerabilities: **not applicable to this diff** — no `package.json` / `package-lock.json` change (`git diff --stat -- '*package.json' '*lock*'` is empty). Nothing new introduced; existing repo residuals are already tracked.\n- Secrets: `trufflehog filesystem` over all touched dirs + the new doc trees — **0 verified, 0 unverified**. Diff grep for key/token/secret/password patterns — clean.\n- SAST: `semgrep` and `gitleaks` are not installed in this environment — skipped per the command's \"skip gracefully\" rule.\n\n**Manual (comparative + OWASP A01–A10 over the changed lines)**\n- **A01 / A04 — trust boundary:** the new check is client-side only and the change does not touch `processPurchase` or any `buy` error handling, so the server stays authoritative and the `InsufficientBalance` handler remains the real enforcement point. Correctly positioned as a UX guard, and the code says so (`// when the balance is unknown, the server remains the guard`). The rule **fails open** when the balance is unknown — the right call here: \"fail secure\" on a non-boundary would mean bricking the Buy button whenever the balance stream lags, with zero security gain.\n- **A03 — injection/XSS:** the one new string is a static `localize()` literal with no interpolation, rendered through React/quill text nodes (`message` prop, `addSnackbar({ message })`). No `dangerouslySetInnerHTML`, no template sinks.\n- **A05 / A09 — info leak:** the message deliberately omits the balance figure, so no account value is echoed into a snackbar, DOM, or log. Nothing new is logged.\n- **A08 / financial:** no money *arithmetic* is introduced — two `Number()` parses and one `>` comparison, which is precision-safe for equal decimal representations and matches how stake bounds are already compared repo-wide. The philosophy's \"never floating point for money\" targets accumulation/rounding, not a single comparison. No new integrity risk.\n- **A02, A06, A07, A10:** no crypto, no deps, no auth/session code, no server-side requests in the diff.\n\n**Passed checks:** server-side enforcement preserved · no secrets · no injection sink · no PII/balance disclosure in error copy · no dependency surface change · no auth/session logic touched · error copy identical for demo and real accounts (no account-type oracle).\n\n---\n\n## CODE REVIEW (staff-engineer persona)\n\n### Verdict: ⚠️ NEEDS CHANGES (no blockers)\n\n### Critical Issues (must fix)\nNone. I traced every candidate to a concrete path and none survived:\n- *Does the desktop wipe actually happen?* Yes — validation runs before the first `debouncedProposal()` at init and before every subsequent one via `onChange`/`onChangeMultiple`; the balance reaction explicitly re-requests on transitions. The one gap I looked for (error appearing mid-stream with no following `requestProposal`, leaving `info.id` set and desktop Buy enabled) has no reachable trigger: both inputs to the rule — `amount` and `balance` — have a path that re-requests.\n- *Can `Object.values(validation_errors).some(errors => errors.length > 0)` throw?* No — `setValidationErrorMessages` only ever stores arrays, and stale keys are deleted by `validateAllProperties`.\n- *Does the wider AppV2 gate disable Buy in states that previously worked?* No — any non-empty `validation_errors` already forced the `requestProposal()` wipe, so those states were \"enabled but payout-less and clicking hung on `when()`\". Strictly better now.\n\n### Recommendations (should fix)\n\n- **Rec-1 · Zero-balance and under-funded users lose the \"Deposit now\" path.** With `balance = 0` (or any balance below the default stake), the trade page now boots straight into disabled Buy buttons + an error snackbar, and the buy request is never sent — so `ServiceErrorSheet`'s `InsufficientBalance` branch, which carries the **`Deposit now`** primary action (`service-error-sheet.tsx:36-56`), becomes unreachable in the common case (only the render-vs-click race still hits it). A brand-new funded-account-less user is a very common state, and this trades a CTA for a dead end. → Either add a deposit affordance to this specific error, or get an explicit product sign-off that \"block + explain\" without a CTA is acceptable. Confidence: 90 (behaviour change is certain; whether it's a defect is a product call).\n- **Rec-2 · Stake sheet still quotes the payout for an over-balance amount.** `useFetchProposalData` is not gated on `insufficient_balance_error`, so `StakeDetails` renders the real payout (the reported `7.69`) directly under the red \"Your stake exceeds your available balance.\" — the sheet's other error states show `-` because their proposal actually failed, so this one reads inconsistently and reproduces the issue's own symptom text. → Cheapest fix in-pattern: `is_empty={!proposal_request_values.amount || !!insufficient_balance_error}` on `StakeDetails` (`stake-input.tsx:446`), which already routes every value to `'-'`. Confidence: 90.\n- **Rec-3 · The balance rule is implemented twice with divergent conditions (DRY).** `validation-rules.ts:22-35` gates on `is_logged_in && basis !== 'payout'`; `stake-input.tsx:176-181` gates on `is_logged_in` only, and the comparison plus the message literal are re-typed. Harmless today only because AppV2 force-switches `basis` to `stake` (`purchase-button.tsx:148-155`) — an implicit dependency nothing in either file records. → Extract one predicate (e.g. `isStakeAboveBalance(amount, client, basis)` beside the other trade-param helpers) and call it from both; keep `localize()` at both call sites if you want the translation key shared. Confidence: 85.\n- **Rec-4 · Four of the eleven new tests have neither a cited red nor a declared characterization exception**, which `framework/tdd-evidence.md` calls \"incomplete\" and the persona checklist flags explicitly. Plan §12 records 5 reds + 1 characterization; the diff adds 11 tests. Unrecorded:\n  - `trade-store.spec.ts` — *\"should clear the amount validation error on logout…\"* (would have been red: no reaction existed)\n  - `trade-error-snackbar.spec.tsx` — *\"calls useSnackbar for a store validation error even when should_show_snackbar is false\"* (red: the bypass didn't exist)\n  - `trade-error-snackbar.spec.tsx` — *\"does not call useSnackbar for a subtype-scoped proposal error…\"* (characterization / suppression guard — passes before and after)\n  - `stake.spec.tsx` — *\"marks the Stake trade param as errored for a store validation error on Rise/Fall\"* (red) and *\"keeps the Stake trade param neutral when only one Rise/Fall subtype has a proposal error\"* (characterization)\n  \n  → Add these five entries to plan §12 before the commit body is written; the two guards must be labelled characterization, not presented as red→green. Confidence: 100 (verifiable from §12 vs. the diff). Non-blocking because it is a record gap, not a code defect — but it must land in the commit/PR body.\n\n### Observations (consider)\n- Broadening the AppV2 gate to **all** `validation_errors` means a field with no AppV2 surface disables Buy with no visible reason. `duration` is the concrete case: it is absent from `TErrorFields` (`useTradeError.ts:6`), so neither the snackbar nor a tile would explain it. Still an improvement over \"enabled but hangs on click\", but widening `TErrorFields` is a sensible follow-up.\n- The balance reaction is not gated on `is_trade_component_mounted`, so an *error-clearing* transition off the trade page (a settlement raising the balance while the user is on contract details) will re-subscribe proposals and repaint barriers. Pre-existing in kind — `trade-store.ts:1277` fires `debouncedProposal()` unguarded too — so I'm not flagging it as introduced.\n- `client.balance` is rehydrated from `localStorage` at boot (`client-store.js:419-424`), so a stale stored balance can briefly drive the rule before the subscription confirms. Self-corrects on the first differing tick; worth knowing when triaging a \"wrong error on refresh\" report.\n- `Object.values(validation_errors).some(...)` now exists in two places — `purchase-button.tsx:122` and `purchase.tsx:63` (`isLoading`). A shared `has_validation_errors` getter on the store would suit a future cleanup.\n- `.spec-to-pr/*.state` is tool scratch state and is not gitignored — stage only `docs/spec-to-pr/` per the plan, or add an ignore entry.\n\n### What's Good\n- **The fix rides existing architecture instead of inventing a parallel one.** One `custom` rule in the same tuple idiom as `barrier_1`, and the whole downstream chain (validation → proposal wipe → disabled Buy → inline message) comes for free on desktop with zero component changes. That is the cheap, boring solution.\n- **The implementation found and fixed a real gap the plan missed.** The plan would have shipped silently-dead Buy buttons on Rise/Fall and Digits; the `is_validation_error` bypass (with the reasoning written down at all three touch points) is exactly the right narrow fix, and it comes with a *negative* test proving the single-subtype suppression it bypasses is still intact. That negative test is the most valuable one in the diff.\n- **Deriving `insufficient_balance_error` at render** rather than in an input handler is the correct call — an event-handler check would miss both a pre-filled over-balance stake and a balance that moves while the sheet is open.\n- **The logout ordering comment** on the reaction (`loginid` clears before `current_account`, so the login flag flips first) is the kind of note that saves the next person an hour, and it is backed by a test rather than just asserted.\n- Comments explain *why*, not *what*, throughout — including why the re-request fires only on transitions.\n- Bidirectional testing (error appears **and** clears) plus the equal-stake boundary; the characterization test for the boundary was honestly labelled in §12.\n\n---\n\nNothing in this pass modified code, and no gates were run beyond the two read-only security scans noted above.\n\n- (no blocking findings)\n\nVERDICT: APPROVED"
  }
